[Term]
id: GSSO:000064
name: histotrophic viviparity
def: "Mode of reproduction in which the zygotes develop in the female's oviducts, but find their nutrients from other tissues, whether skin or glandular tissue, oophagy, or adelphophagy (intra-uterine cannibalism in some sharks or in the black salamander Salamandra atra)." []
is_a: GSSO:000063 ! viviparity
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy http://doi.org/10.1016/j.repbio.2012.09.001

[Term]
id: GSSO:000065
name: hemotrophic viviparity
def: "Mode of reproduction in which nutrients are provided by the female, often through a placenta, as in mammals (except for marsupials and monotremes). In the frog, Gastrotheca ovifera, embryos are fed by the mother through specialized gills. The lizard Pseudomoia pagenstecheri and most mammals use this form of viviparity." []
is_a: GSSO:000063 ! viviparity
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy http://doi.org/10.1016/j.repbio.2012.09.001

[Term]
id: GSSO:000066
name: ovuliparity
def: "Mode of reproduction in which fertilization is external, with the oocytes being released into the environment and fertilized outside the female's body by the male. This is common in molluscs, arthropods, and fishes, and is found in most frogs." []
is_a: GSSO:000062 ! reproductive mode
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy http://doi.org/10.1016/j.repbio.2012.09.001

