is_a: GSSO:003858 ! second-wave feminism
property_value: Property:P5191 GSSO:002229
property_value: terms:replaces "bourgeois feminism" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:002246
name: Marxist feminism
def: "A philosophical variant of feminism. Marxist Feminism is focused on the ways in which women could be oppressed through systems of capitalism and the individual ownership of private property. According to Marxist feminists, women's liberation can only be achieved through a radical restructuring of capitalist economies, in which, they contend, much of women's labor is uncompensated." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marxist_feminism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1321958
is_a: GSSO:002241 ! gender reform feminism
is_a: GSSO:003858 ! second-wave feminism
property_value: Property:P5191 GSSO:002229

[Term]
id: GSSO:002247
name: socialist feminism
def: "A branch of feminism which rose in the 1960s and 1970s as an offshoot of the feminist movement and New Left that focuses upon the interconnectivity of the patriarchy and capitalism. Socialist feminists argue that liberation can only be achieved by working to end both the economic and cultural sources of women's oppression." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Socialist_feminism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Socialist_feminism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2420666
is_a: GSSO:002241 ! gender reform feminism
is_a: GSSO:003858 ! second-wave feminism
property_value: Property:P5191 GSSO:002229
property_value: wiki:Q1823134 "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008005196" xsd:anyURI

[Term]
id: GSSO:002248
name: lesbian feminism
def: "Lesbian feminism, much like feminism, lesbian and gay studies, and queer theory, is characterized by the ideas of contestation and revision. At the same time, one of the key themes of lesbian feminism is the analysis of heterosexuality as an institution. Lesbian feminist texts work to denaturalise heterosexuality and, based on this denaturalization, to explore heterosexuality's \"roots\" in institutions such as patriarchy, capitalism, and colonialism. Additionally, lesbian feminism advocates lesbianism as a rational result of alienation and dissatisfaction with these institutions." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Lesbian_feminism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lesbian_feminism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q18299
