[Term]
id: GSSO:009022
name: restorative justice
def: "An approach to justice in which one of the responses to a crime is to organize a meeting between the victim and the offender, sometimes with representatives of the wider community. The goal is for them to share their experience of what happened, to discuss who was harmed by the crime and how, and to create a consensus for what the offender can do to repair the harm from the offense. This may include a payment of money given from the offender to the victim, apologies and other amends, and other actions to compensate those affected and to prevent the offender from causing future harm." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Restorative_justice
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Restorative_justice
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1165392
is_a: GSSO:003240 ! justice
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy GSSO:011100
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy http://web.archive.org/web/20200721053627/https://sacred.omeka.net/glossary
property_value: wiki:Q1823134 "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh99004087" xsd:anyURI

[Term]
id: GSSO:009023
name: environmental racism
def: "A concept in the environmental justice movement, which developed in the United States throughout the 1970s and 1980s. The term is used to describe environmental injustice that occurs within a racialized context both in practice and policy. In the United States, environmental racism criticizes inequalities between urban and exurban areas after white flight. Internationally, environmental racism can refer to the effects of the global waste trade, like the negative health impact of the export of electronic waste to China from developed countries." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_racism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2491965
is_a: GSSO:002798 ! racism
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy http://web.archive.org/web/20200721053627/https://sacred.omeka.net/glossary

[Term]
id: GSSO:009024
name: endemic racism
def: "Racism which is endemic and especially widespread within a particular society, group, and/or area." []
is_a: GSSO:002798 ! racism
property_value: terms:isReferencedBy http://web.archive.org/web/20200721053627/https://sacred.omeka.net/glossary

[Term]
id: GSSO:009025
name: Afro-Mexican
def: "A Mexican person of predominantly Sub-Saharan African descent and heritage. As a single population, Afro-Mexicans includes individuals descended from Black slaves brought to Mexico during the colonial era in the transatlantic African slave trade, as well as others of more recent immigrant African descent, including Afro-descended persons from neighboring English, French, and Spanish-speaking countries of the Caribbean and Central America, descendants of fugitive slaves who escaped to Mexico from the Southern United States, and to a lesser extent recent immigrants directly from Africa." []
def: "Note that the term \"Blaxican\" should only be used by members identifying within that community." []
comment: More broadly, this term (and its synonyms) may include persons identifying as both African American and Mexican American.
is_a: GSSO:003731 ! Black people by nationality
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "Black Mexican" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="Black Mexicans"}
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "Blaxican" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="Blaxicans"}
property_value: schema:alternateName "Afro-Mexicans" xsd:string

