[Term]
id: GSSO:009071
name: Black impersonation
def: "Almost always offensive impersonation of a Black person in any of various forms. Nearly exclusively a form of cultural appropriation and discrimination." []
is_a: GSSO:009070 ! racial impersonation

[Term]
id: GSSO:009072
name: Blackface
def: "A term used to describe a form of theatrical make-up and impersonation style used predominantly by non-Black performers to represent a caricature of a Black person. It is almost always considered to be extremely offensive." []
comment: In the United States the practice gained popularity during the 19th century and contributed to the spread of racial stereotypes such as the "happy-go-lucky darky on the plantation" or the "dandified coon". By the middle of the century, Blackface minstrel shows had become a distinctive American artform, translating formal works such as opera into popular terms for a general audience. Early in the 20th century, Blackface branched off from the minstrel show and became a form in its own right. In the United States, Blackface had largely fallen out of favor by the turn of the 21st century, and is now generally considered offensive, disrespectful, and racist, though the practice continues in other countries.
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blackface
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q880808
is_a: GSSO:009071 ! Black impersonation
property_value: wiki:Q1823134 "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2019005112" xsd:anyURI

[Term]
id: GSSO:009073
name: digital Blackface
def: "A form of Blackface involving any of various digital media, in which persons utilize or directly impersonate Black personages (whether it be mimicking a \"Black\" manner of speaking, usage of Black emojis or Black reaction GIFs, as well as full-on pretending to be a Black person)." []
is_a: GSSO:009072 ! Blackface
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "high-tech Blackface" xsd:string
property_value: wiki:Q1823134 "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2019005113" xsd:anyURI

