[Term]
id: GSSO:009499
name: burlesque show
def: "A kind of variety show derived from elements of Victorian burlesque, music hall and minstrel shows. Burlesque shows in America became popular in the late 1860s and slowly evolved to feature ribald comedy and female nudity. By the early 1930s the striptease element of burlesque overshadowed the comedy and subjected burlesque to extensive local legislation. Burlesque gradually lost popularity beginning in the 1940s. A number of producers sought to capitalize on nostalgia for the entertainment by attempting to recreate the spirit of burlesque in Hollywood films from the 1930s to the 1960s. There has been a resurgence of interest in this format since the 1990s." []
is_a: GSSO:009498 ! variety show
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "burlesque performance" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="burlesque performances"}
property_value: Property:P1813 "burlesque" xsd:string
property_value: Property:P2283 GSSO:009501
property_value: schema:alternateName "burlesque shows" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:009500
name: cabaret show
def: "A kind of variety show featuring music, song, dance, recitation, or drama. It is mainly distinguished by the performance venue, which might be a pub, a casino, a hotel, a restaurant, or a nightclub with a stage for performances. The audience, often dining or drinking, does not typically dance but usually sits at tables. Performances are usually introduced by a master of ceremonies or MC." []
is_a: GSSO:009498 ! variety show
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "cabaret performance" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="cabaret performances"}
property_value: Property:P1813 "cabaret" xsd:string
