[Term]
id: GSSO:010037
name: Minoan Brotherhood
def: "A Minoan tradition founded in 1975 by Edmund Buczynski, an elder in the Gardnerian, WICA, and New York Welsh Traditions, in order to create a tradition for gay and bisexual men—one that would celebrate and explore the distinctive mysteries unique to men who love men." []
is_a: GSSO:010031 ! LGBTQ neopaganism
is_a: GSSO:010036 ! Modern Minoan paganism

[Term]
id: GSSO:010038
name: Minoan Sisterhood
def: "A Minoan tradition founded in 1976 by Lady Rhea and Lady Miw-Sekhmet, in collaboration with Edmund Buczynski, as the women's counterpart to the Minoan Brotherhood." []
is_a: GSSO:010031 ! LGBTQ neopaganism
is_a: GSSO:010036 ! Modern Minoan paganism

[Term]
id: GSSO:010039
name: Fellowship of the Phoenix
def: "A queer neopagan tradition founded in the summer of 2004 by seven gay men from diverse traditions such as ceremonial magic, shamanism, and pre-Gardnerian witchcraft in order to create an ecumenical neopagan tradition which serves the community of men who love men. In 2017, the Seattle Temple began a reformation within the group to expand the tradition to be \"open to all queer/LBGTQIA adults\" which has been accepted throughout. Fellowship theology has been modified to fit an expanded, inclusive model." []
is_a: GSSO:010031 ! LGBTQ neopaganism

[Term]
id: GSSO:010040
name: Goddess religion
def: "Any of various spiritual beliefs or practices (chiefly neopagan) which emerged predominantly in North America, Western Europe, Australia, and New Zealand in the 1970s, which arose from perceptions of predominant organized religion as male-dominated. Such religions usually make use of goddess worship and have a focus on gender and femininity." []
