[Term]
id: GSSO:010119
name: prescriptive linguistics
def: "The attempt to establish rules defining preferred or correct usage of language. These rules may address such linguistic aspects as spelling, pronunciation, vocabulary, syntax, and semantics. Sometimes informed by linguistic purism, such normative practices may suggest that some usages are incorrect, inconsistent, illogical, lack communicative effect, or are of low aesthetic value. They may also include judgments on socially proper and politically correct language use." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linguistic_prescription
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1321978
is_a: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MESH/D008037 ! linguistics
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "linguistic prescription" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:010120
name: Aesthetic movement
def: "An intellectual and art movement supporting the emphasis of aesthetic values more than social-political themes for literature, fine art, music and other arts. This meant that art from this particular movement focused more on being beautiful rather than having a deeper meaning — \"art for art's sake\". It was particularly prominent in England during the late 19th century, supported by notable figures such as Walter Pater and Oscar Wilde." []
synonym: "aesthetic movement" EXACT []
synonym: "Aestheticism" RELATED [] {terms:isReferencedBy="https://books.google.com/books?id=-kltQgAACAAJ"}
synonym: "art for art's sake" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aestheticism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q256922
