[Term]
id: GSSO:010225
name: conservatism
def: "A political and social philosophy promoting traditional social institutions in the context of culture and civilization. The central tenets of conservatism include tradition, hierarchy, and authority, as established in respective cultures, as well as property rights. Conservatives seek to preserve a range of institutions such as organized religion, parliamentary government, and property rights, with the aim of emphasizing continuity. Adherents of conservatism often oppose modernism and seek a return to \"the way things were\"." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Conservatism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservatism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7169
is_a: GSSO:002225 ! political ideology
property_value: dc:identifier "https://homosaurus.org/terms/conservatism" xsd:anyURI {terms:source="http://web.archive.org/web/20160404165237/https://homosaurus.org/terms"}
property_value: wiki:Q1823134 "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85031272" xsd:anyURI

[Term]
id: GSSO:010226
name: American conservatism
def: "A political ideology characterized by respect for American traditions, republicanism, limited government, support for Christian values, moral universalism, pro-business, opposition to trade unions, strong national defense, free trade, protectionism, anti-communism, rugged individualism, advocacy of American exceptionalism, and a defense of tradition and Western culture from the perceived threats posed by communism, socialism, and moral relativism." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Conservatism_in_the_United_States
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservatism_in_the_United_States
