[Term]
id: GSSO:011028
name: lexical semantics
def: "A subfield of linguistic semantics. The units of analysis in lexical semantics are lexical units which include not only words but also sub-words or sub-units such as affixes and even compound words and phrases. Lexical units include the catalogue of words in a language, the lexicon. Lexical semantics looks at how the meaning of the lexical units correlates with the structure of the language or syntax. This is referred to as syntax-semantic interface." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Lexical_semantics
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexical_semantics
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1759657
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology
is_a: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MESH/D012660 ! semantics
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "lexicosemantics" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:011029
name: general linguistics
def: "A term often utilized in higher education contexts in reference to subfields of linguistics considered to be \"core\" to the field as a whole." []
is_a: http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MESH/D008037 ! linguistics

[Term]
id: GSSO:011030
name: lexicography
def: "The study of language lexicons, either in a practical sense or in a theoretical sense, in relationship to dictionaries." []
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology

[Term]
id: GSSO:011031
name: practical lexicography
def: "The art or craft of compiling, writing and editing dictionaries." []
is_a: GSSO:011030 ! lexicography
property_value: Property:P1813 "lexicography" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:011032
name: theoretical lexicography
def: "The scholarly discipline of describing the semantic, orthographic, syntagmatic, and paradigmatic features of lexemes of the lexicon (vocabulary) of a language, developing theories of dictionary components and structures linking the data in dictionaries, the needs for information by users in specific types of situations, and how users may best access the data incorporated in printed and electronic dictionaries." []
is_a: GSSO:011030 ! lexicography
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "metalexicography" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:011033
name: lexicology
def: "The branch of linguistics that analyzes the lexicon of a specific language." []
is_a: GSSO:011029 ! general linguistics

[Term]
id: GSSO:011034
name: general lexicology
def: "The broad study of words regardless of a language's specific properties. It is concerned with linguistic features that are common among all languages, such as phonemes and morphemes." []
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology

[Term]
id: GSSO:011035
name: special lexicology
def: "A subfield of lexicology which looks at what a particular language contributes to its vocabulary, such as grammars." []
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology

[Term]
id: GSSO:011036
name: diachronic lexicology
def: "A subfield of lexicology which is devoted to the evolution of words and word-formation over time. It investigates the origins of a word and the ways in which its structure, meaning, and usage have since changed." []
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "historical lexicology" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:011037
name: synchronic lexicology
def: "A subfield of lexicology which examines the words of a language within a certain time frame. This could be a period during the language's early stages of development, its current state, or any given interval in between." []
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology

[Term]
id: GSSO:011038
name: phraseology
def: "The study of set or fixed expressions, such as idioms, phrasal verbs, and other types of multi-word lexical units (often collectively referred to as phrasemes), in which the component parts of the expression take on a meaning more specific than or otherwise not predictable from the sum of their meanings when used independently." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Phraseology
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phraseology
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1126894
is_a: GSSO:011033 ! lexicology

[Term]
id: GSSO:011039
name: semasiology
def: "A subdiscipline of linguistics concerned with the question \"what does the word X mean?\". It studies the meaning of words regardless how they are pronounced." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semasiology
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1931606
