[Term]
id: GSSO:011156
name: populism
def: "A term which refers to a range of political stances that emphasize the idea of \"the people\" and often juxtapose this group against \"the elite\". The term developed in the 19th century and has been applied to various politicians, parties, and movements since that time, although it has rarely been chosen as a self-description. Within political science and other social sciences, several different definitions of populism have been employed, with some scholars proposing that the term be rejected altogether. A common framework for interpreting populism is known as the ideational approach: this defines populism as an ideology which presents \"the people\" as a morally good force and contrasts them against \"the elite\", who are portrayed as corrupt and self-serving. Populists differ in how \"the people\" are defined, but it can be based along class, ethnic, or national lines. Populists typically present \"the elite\" as comprising the political, economic, cultural, and media establishment, depicted as a homogeneous entity and accused of placing their own interests, and often the interests of other groups—such as large corporations, foreign countries, or immigrants—above the interests of \"the people\"." []
synonym: "populist" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Populism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Populism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q180490
is_a: GSSO:002225 ! political ideology
property_value: wiki:Q1823134 "http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85104931" xsd:anyURI

[Term]
id: GSSO:011157
name: elitism
def: "The belief or notion that individuals who form an elite—a select group of people perceived as having an intrinsic quality, high intellect, wealth, power, notablity, special skills, or experience—are more likely to be constructive to society as a whole, and therefore deserve influence or authority greater than that of others." []
synonym: "elitist" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elitism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q422933
is_a: GSSO:002225 ! political ideology

[Term]
id: GSSO:011158
name: right-wing populism
def: "A political ideology which combines right-wing politics and populist rhetoric and themes. The rhetoric often consists of anti-elitist sentiments, opposition to the Establishment, and speaking to the \"common people\". Both right-wing populism and left-wing populism object to the perceived control of liberal democracies by elites; however, populism of the left also objects to the power of large corporations and their allies, while populism of the right normally supports strong controls on immigration." []
synonym: "national populism" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Right-wing_populism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right-wing_populism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q436860
is_a: GSSO:011156 ! populism

[Term]
id: GSSO:011159
name: left-wing populism
def: "A political ideology that combines left-wing politics with populist rhetoric and themes. Its rhetoric often consists of anti-elitism, opposition to the Establishment and speaking for the \"common people\". Recurring themes for left-wing populists include economic democracy, social justice, and skepticism of globalization. Socialist theory plays a lesser role than in traditional left-wing ideologies." []
def: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left-wing_populism" []
def: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11900614" []
synonym: "social populism" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Left-wing_populism
is_a: GSSO:011156 ! populism

[Term]
id: GSSO:011160
name: paleoconservatism
def: "A political philosophy and variety of conservatism in the United States stressing American nationalism, Christian ethics, regionalism, and traditionalist conservatism. Paleoconservatism's concerns overlap with those of the Old Right that opposed the New Deal in the 1930s and 1940s as well as with paleolibertarianism and right-wing populism." []
synonym: "paleoconservative" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Paleoconservatism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paleoconservatism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1074954
is_a: GSSO:010226 ! American conservatism
property_value: Property:P1813 "paleocon" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:011161
name: neoconservatism
def: "A political movement born in the United States during the 1960s among liberal hawks who became disenchanted with the increasingly pacifist foreign policy of the Democratic Party and with the growing New Left and counterculture of the 1960s, particularly the Vietnam protests. Some also began to question their liberal beliefs regarding domestic policies such as the Great Society. Neoconservatives typically advocate the promotion of democracy and interventionism in international affairs, including peace through strength, and are known for espousing disdain for communism and political radicalism." []
synonym: "neoconservative" RELATED []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Neoconservatism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoconservatism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q192139
is_a: GSSO:010226 ! American conservatism
property_value: Property:P1813 "neocon" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:011162
name: Neo-Confederatism
def: "Any of various ideologies which portray the Confederate States of America and its actions during the American Civil War in a positive light. Some neo-Confederate organizations such as the League of the South continue to advocate the secession of the former Confederate States." []
synonym: "Neo-Confederacy" RELATED []
synonym: "Neo-Confederate" RELATED [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo-Confederate, https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6991715]
is_a: GSSO:002289 ! neo-fascism
is_a: GSSO:011160 ! paleoconservatism

[Term]
id: GSSO:011163
name: Neo-Confederate historical revisionism
def: "Actions and processes often undertaken by Neo-Confederates and Neo-Confederate-related or -aligned entities to present a version of the American Civil War in which secession was not rebellion, the Confederacy did not fight for slavery, and the Confederate soldier was defeated by overwhelming numbers and resources." []
is_a: GSSO:009052 ! historical revisionism

