[Term]
id: GSSO:012592
name: pajamafication
def: "A subtle form of dehumanization in which fiction literature is used to teach history in schools; for instance, using literature which is said to be \"age-appropriate\" in order to propagate a less horrific version of historical events, or to otherwise position others living through those atrocities as \"historically innocent\"." []
comment: Named after the book The Boy in the Striped Pajamas, by John Boyne, following the banning of the book Maus by the McMinn County, Tennessee, Board of Education. Autho Gwen C. Katz noted that "[t]he Boy in the Striped Pajamas is tailor-made for classroom use. It's taught at countless schools and it's squeaky-clean of any of the parent-objectionable material you might find in Maus, Night, or any of the other first-person accounts of the Holocaust... It's also a terrible way to teach the Holocaust." Katz goes on to state several historical issues with the portrayal set forward in The Boy in the Striped Pajamas, saying "The Boy in the Striped Pajamas turns a specific historical atrocity into a parable about all forms of bigotry and injustice. I'm sure Boyne thinks he's being very profound. But the actual effect is to blunt and erase the atrocity."
is_a: GSSO:003432 ! dehumanization
property_value: Property:P3938 "Gwen C. Katz" xsd:string {dc:date="2022-01-29T03:57:00"}

