[Term]
id: GSSO:012663
name: differential K theory
def: "A scientific racist theory, first proposed by Canadian psychologist J. Philippe Rushton in 1985, which attempts to apply r/K selection theory to human races. According to Rushton, this theory explains race differences in fertility, IQ, criminality, and sexual anatomy and behavior. The theory also hypothesizes that a single factor, the \"K factor\", affects multiple population statistics Rushton referred to as \"life-history traits\"." []
comment: Many researchers have criticized Rushton's theory. It has been argued that the predictions based on the theory "are supported by selective citation and misrepresentation of the research literature and by the use of unreliable sources" and that Rushton's methodology "indicates a lack of familiarity with ecological thinking and scientific method in general." A 2014 study found differences in the General Factor of Personality across races that were not compatible with differential K theory.
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_K_theory
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q28455595
is_a: GSSO:012662 ! scientific racist theory

[Term]
id: GSSO:012664
name: Jensenist theory
def: "A scientific racist theory, named after educational psychologist Arthur Jensen, which was originally defined as \"the theory that IQ is largely determined by the genes\". However, because Jensen and proponents often used this theory to argue scientifically racist thought, it became associated with the theory that Black persons are genetically mentally inferior to white persons, based on the results of IQ tests." []
comment: Contemporary scientific consensus has shown that genetic factors do not  explain IQ differences between socially constructed racial groups. Despite this, a number of prominent evolutionary psychologists still preach Jensenism under other labels such as "race realism" or "human biodiversity". For instance, The Bell Curve, by Richard Herrnstein and Charles Murray claimed to support Jensenism. It is also notable that a number of far-right and alt-right scholars and organizations continue to fuel Jensenist beliefs in the 21st century. One such organization, the Pioneer Fund, deemed a hate group by the Southern Poverty Law Center, attempted to fund distribution of a Nazi propaganda film about eugenics in U.S. schools and churches. Generally, Jensenism is highly associated with the 21st century "intellectual dark web" of scholars, many of whom have espoused sexist, racist, homophobic, and transphobic beliefs.
synonym: "race and intelligence" RELATED [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Race_and_intelligence, https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3041074]
synonym: "race and intelligence controversy" RELATED [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Race_and_intelligence_controversy]
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jensenism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60775437
is_a: GSSO:012662 ! scientific racist theory
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "Jensenism" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:012665
name: Aryanism
def: "A white supremacist ideology which views the supposed Aryan race as a distinct and superior racial group which is entitled to rule the rest of humanity. Initially promoted by racist theorists such as Arthur de Gobineau and Houston Stewart Chamberlain, Aryanism reached its peak of influence in Nazi Germany. In the 1930s and 40s, the regime applied the ideology with full force, sparking World War II with the 1939 invasion of Poland in pursuit of Lebensraum, or living space, for the Aryan people. The racial policies which were implemented by the Nazis during the 1930s came to a head during their conquest of Europe and the Soviet Union, culminating in the industrial mass murder of six million Jews and eleven million other victims in what is now known as the Holocaust." []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aryanism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Aryanism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q85743247
is_a: GSSO:002292 ! white supremacy

[Term]
id: GSSO:012666
name: Nordicism
def: "A racist and white supremacist ideology which views the \"Nordic race\" as an endangered and superior racial group." []
comment: The notion of the superiority of the "Nordic race" and the superiority of the Northwestern European nations that were associated with this supposed race influenced the United States' Immigration Act of 1924 (which effectively banned or severely limited the immigration of Italians, Jews, and other Southern and Eastern Europeans) and the later Immigration and Nationality Act of 1952, and it was also present in other countries outside Northwestern Europe and the United States, such as Australia, Canada, and South Africa. By the 1930s, the Nazis claimed that the Nordic race was the most superior branch of the "Aryan race" and constituted a master race (Herrenvolk). The full application of this belief system—the invasion of Poland and further conquest in the pursuit of Lebensraum, 'living space'—was the immediate catalyst for World War II and led directly to the industrial mass murder of six million Jews and eleven million other victims in what is now known as the Holocaust.
synonym: "Anglo-Saxonism" NARROW [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/19th-century_Anglo-Saxonism, https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q85727707] {hasSynonym="racial Anglo-Saxonism", hasSynonym="19th-century Anglo-Saxonism"}
synonym: "Fascist Nordicism" NARROW []
synonym: "Frankisism" NARROW []
synonym: "Nazi Nordicism" NARROW []
synonym: "Teutonicism" NARROW []
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category\:Nordicism
xref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nordicism
xref: https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q4327314
is_a: GSSO:002292 ! white supremacy

[Term]
id: GSSO:012667
name: racial degeneration theory
def: "A subtype of degeneration theory which posited that racial desegregation, the abolition of slavery, racial equality and equity, racial civil rights, and other societal changes were somehow connected to societal collapse." []
is_a: GSSO:002362 ! degeneration theory
is_a: GSSO:012662 ! scientific racist theory
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "racial degeneration" xsd:string
property_value: schema:alternateName "racial degeneration theories" xsd:string
property_value: seeAlso GSSO:006479

[Term]
id: GSSO:012668
name: sexual degeneration theory
def: "A subtype of degeneration theory which posited that changing sexual or gender norms, homosexuality, \"sexual inversion\", transness, gender equality, feminism, BDSM, etc., were somehow leading to the collapse of society." []
comment: The very idea of "sexual degeneracy" has been stretched to inane conclusions, with those in the 19th and 20th believing that allowing women to vote would even constitute the collapse of society, or that homosexuality had something to do with the collapse of the Roman Empire, which is demonstrably false. However, despite such theories being bizarre, they are still published commonly in the 21st century. For instance, The Guardian published an article "How same-sex marriage could ruin civilisation" in 2015. A 2011 Telegraph article claimed that the "Fall of the Roman Empire caused by 'contagion of homosexuality'". And, in 2022, Jordan Peterson claimed that transgender people were somehow evidence of societal collapse.
is_a: GSSO:002362 ! degeneration theory
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "sex degeneration theory" xsd:string
property_value: schema:alternateName "sexual degeneration theories" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:012669
name: race proxy theory
def: "Any of various theories which utilize race as a proxy for other characteristics. For instance, calling race a social determinant of health, rather than racism, the actual underlying social determinant. The usage of race corrections in health care and \"race-norming\" could also be considered forms of race proxy theory. However, the original usage of the phrase \"race proxy theory\" was to describe how white people often use neighborhood racial composition as a proxy for various \"social problems\" (such as crime)." []
synonym: "race proxy hypothesis" RELATED [] {schema:alternateName="race proxy hypotheses"}
is_a: GSSO:012662 ! scientific racist theory
property_value: schema:alternateName "race proxy theories" xsd:string

[Term]
id: GSSO:012670
name: theory of race correction
def: "Any of various theories which utilize race as a proxy to \"correct\" or \"adjust\" some value. Theories of race correction have been utilized widely in health care, and have also been used to adjust test scores. Both practices have been connected with and compared to eugenics and pseudoscientific racism, because the supposed proxy related to these changes is unclear at best, and unduely malicious at worst." []
is_a: GSSO:012669 ! race proxy theory
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "race adjustment theory" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="race adjustment theories"}
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "race correction theory" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="race correction theories"}
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "theory of race adjustment" xsd:string {schema:alternateName="theories of race adjustment"}
property_value: schema:alternateName "theories of race correction" xsd:string

