[Term]
id: GSSO:012745
name: homeopathic medicine
def: "A pseudoscientific system of alternative medicine. Its practitioners, called homeopaths, believe that a substance that causes symptoms of a disease in healthy people can cure similar symptoms in sick people; this doctrine is called similia similibus curentur, or \"like cures like\". All relevant scientific knowledge about physics, chemistry, biochemistry and biology gained since at least the mid-19th century contradicts homeopathy. Homeopathic remedies are biochemically inert, and have no effect on any known disease." []
is_a: GSSO:012746 ! naturopathic medicine
property_value: dc:identifier "https://homosaurus.org/terms/homeopathy" xsd:anyURI {terms:source="http://web.archive.org/web/20160404165237/https://homosaurus.org/terms"}
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "homeopathy" xsd:string {xref="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Homeopathy", xref="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homeopathy", xref="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q81058", hasSynonym="homoeopathy", wiki:Q1823134="http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85061725", Property:P1149="RX", Property:P3201="http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MEDDRA/10020363", Property:P486="http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MESH/D006705"}

[Term]
id: GSSO:012746
name: naturopathic medicine
def: "A form of alternative medicine. It employs an array of pseudoscientific practices branded as \"natural\", \"non-invasive\", or promoting \"self-healing\". The practices of naturopaths, the practitioners of naturopathic medicine, vary widely and are difficult to generalize. he ideology and methods of naturopathy are based on vitalism and folk medicine rather than evidence-based medicine (EBM), although some practitioners may use techniques supported by EBM. Naturopathic practitioners commonly recommend against following modern medical practices, including but not limited to medical testing, drugs, vaccinations, and surgery. Instead, naturopathic practice relies on unscientific notions, often leading naturopaths to diagnoses and treatments that have no factual merit." []
is_a: GSSO:009170 ! pseudomedicine
property_value: oboInOwl:hasSynonym "naturopathy" xsd:string {xref="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Naturopathy", xref="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naturopathy", xref="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q213403", Property:P3201="http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MEDDRA/10066398", Property:P486="http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/MESH/D009324", Property:P5806="http://purl.bioontology.org/ontology/SNOMEDCT/439809005"}

